# Which schools attack this problem, and why.
#
# All three run here, and this is the one problem where that is affordable
# reasoning rather than indecision: the real solution is a long ladder in
# cyclotomic fields that no thirty-minute attempt is going to climb, so the
# question this run answers is not *does it solve it* but *what does each school
# do when it cannot*. That is a comparison, and it needs the full set.
#
# `chisel` computes: it will verify (3,2,2,3) is alone in a bounded range and
# has to decide whether to keep enlarging the range. Whether it does is the
# `computational` threshold under test.
#
# `rising-sea` should leave the integers — the actual proof does, into
# cyclotomic fields — and the question is whether it names a setting or merely
# gestures at one.
#
# `adversarial` should price what is not proved, which on a problem with one
# known solution and an astronomical effective bound is the most informative
# thing anyone can honestly do. If it produces a defensible statement of exactly
# what remains open, that is a result even though the conjecture is not touched.
schools=chisel,rising-sea,adversarial
